  "hints": [
    "Read the header with one `fgets` and ignore it. Then loop over the remaining lines with fgets.",
    "`sscanf(line, \"%d,%d\", &time, &alt) == 2` is true only for a good row: `12,` fills one value and `,400` fills none.",
    "Track the peak with `>` (not `>=`) so a later equal altitude doesn't replace the first time. Keep a flag for \"seen a good row yet\" so altitudes of 0 still count."
  ],
  "solution": "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    FILE *f = fopen(\"flight.csv\", \"r\");\n    if (f == NULL) {\n        perror(\"flight.csv\");\n        return 1;\n    }\n    char line[128];\n    if (fgets(line, sizeof line, f) == NULL) {\n        fclose(f);\n        printf(\"rows 0, corrupt 0\\npeak none\\n\");\n        return 0;\n    }\n    int good = 0, bad = 0, peak = 0, peak_time = 0;\n    while (fgets(line, sizeof line, f) != NULL) {\n        int time, alt;\n        if (sscanf(line, \"%d,%d\", &time, &alt) != 2) {\n            bad++;\n            continue;\n        }\n        if (good == 0 || alt > peak) {\n            peak = alt;\n            peak_time = time;\n        }\n        good++;\n    }\n    fclose(f);\n    printf(\"rows %d, corrupt %d\\n\", good, bad);\n    if (good == 0) printf(\"peak none\\n\");\n    else printf(\"peak %d at %d\\n\", peak, peak_time);\n    return 0;\n}\n"
}
